ng's gonna come out and it's  written in a way that you you're not  quite sure if it's actually there or if  it  or if it isn't there um but  uh the short stories range from like 20  pages to  just one page and for me i  really enjoyed the shorter ones uh the  the most like the ones which are just  kind of a snapshot  they were just a moment in what could be  a broader story  uh henley cutter clearly has a really  good grasp of  uh just kind of prose and metaphors  and you know just beautiful poetic  language i've actually got a couple of  lines that i want to i want to share  with you  so these are just ones that you know may  not be the best lines in the entire book  but there are ones that are kind of like  oh  oh i feel that you know i get that uh  so the first one is the house is  brittled  slouching with age and neglect and spite  and i was just like oh i can i can see  that you know i can see that house there  with that  there's another one which is uh a child  was raised on stories of crows  dark creatures with black intentions  that's from one called mother which was  another kind of shorter one  where you know you find out whether the  crows are bad  she writes really beautifully  and i want to say that that the strength  of starlight was definitely in the  atmosphere  in the feeling in the kind of  somber sorrowful but sometimes hopeful  uh kind of grim but still very  grounded world that she she built  throughout all of these stories they all  had a very distinct  feeling to them there was a lot of  atmosphere and mood  those were really where the the book  shone  um for me as i said you know i preferred  some of the  the shorter ones i think that uh the  longer ones if i were to offer  you know a critique here is that coming  from someone who mostly reads  longer novels uh you know the build-up  of tension  to kind of climaxes and whatever it's  very much about  is very much a part of how i process  fiction and  the stories that sort of did that a bit  